goes on to add that unfamiliar words or unnatural structures can be included in the sections
of a translated book where space is limited by pictures or graphics. One reason for this is
because the short simple English words or phrases that fill these areas of the page can have
translations that are either more grammatically complex or longer and more syntantically
complex. In her conclusions De Brún (ibid, p.32) adds that COGG (An Chomhairle um
Oideachas Gaeltachta agus Gaelscolaíochta) have found teachers reporting that translations
of textbooks cause particular problems for learners, and that the level of Irish in them is too
high.
This topic of research continues in Ó Muircheartaigh (2009), where the opinions of teachers
